Explanation on how to use the "prepare_sims.py".

Here I present an easy way to prepare raster simulations for the PP mode in ALF.
To use this, make sure to compile ALF with PP.

Definition raster simulation: A simulation containing at least one varying simulation parameter. 

1. Copy the files from $ALF_DIR/Scripts_and_Parameters_files/Start_PP into your simulation directory.
2. Adjust the "parameters" file according to:
a) For each varying simulation parameter list all values your parameter can have. 
Example: 
...
Beta         = {2, 4, 6, 8}  
...
ham_U      = {0.5d0, 1.5d0, 3.0d0, 5.0d0, 5.5d0, 6.0d0, 7.5d0} 
...

b) There is no need to adjust "mpi_per_parameter_set" in the parameters file, as this will be automatically overwritten by the python script
c) You then can launch the simulation via "mpiexec -n <n_cores> $ALF_DIR/Prog/ALF.out", where <n_cores> = <mpi_per_parameter_set> \times <number_of_mpi_runs_per_parameter_set>. 
Note that I) The number of cores has to be available on your system, II) you really have to use the <mpi_per_parameter_set> times an integer. If <n_cores> mod <mpi_per_parameter_set> \neq 0, ALF will fail.

Some remarks: 
If you use different Beta on a HPC system, make sure to use the CPU_MAX option with some large number of bins. In this example, Beta=2 will be approximately four times faster than Beta=8.
It is therefore in general not advisable to use different \beta in one single production run raster simulation (unless you know what you are doing).
The same is true for different system sizes and different \delta \tau.
The automatic creation works for ALL parameters in "parameters". I.e. also for parameters with value ".T.; .F." and so on. 

Raster over different lattice sizes:
1D Systems:
Set either L1 or L2 = 0. The remaining variable then is set to a set over different lattice sizes {8, 16, 32, 64}.

2D Systems with L1=L2:
Set either L1 = ## or L2 = ##, notice, it does not matter if there is a space. All that matters is, that after "=" there are "##" before a "!" in the same line.
The remaining variable is then set to a set of different lattice sizes, e.g. {8, 12, 16}.

2D Systems with L1\neqL2:
Set each L1 and L2 to some set of different lattice sizes, e.g.:
L1 = {8, 12, 16}
L2 = {2, 4, 6}


Running the script:
Run the script with "python prepare_sims.py".
Upon running the script you will find several folders named "Temp_". These are the folders, that will contain a unique combination of parameters.
Notice also, that links are generated, in order to ensure better distinguishability between the different simulations.

Running the simulation:
Launch the simulation via "mpiexec -n <ncores> $ALF_DIR/Prog/ALF.out"


Special settings:
1. It is also possible, that someone wants to use this setup to not wanting to Launch raster simulations, but rather the following:
a) e.g. Say you want to have a raster simulation:
L1=##
L2={8,12,16}
...
ham_U={0.5d0, 1.5d0, 3.0d0, 5.0d0, 6.0d0, 7.5d0}
...

And you want to have a different \beta for each different Lattice size, i.e. you don't want to have a raster for beta, but a 1:1 mapping for a specific L value.
Then you can fix beta in such a way: 
beta=[4, 6, 8; L2]
This works only, if beta has the same length as (in this case) L2.

b) You can always fix several parameters at once! E.g.:

&VAR_lattice               !! Parameters defining the specific lattice and base model
L1           = ##            ! Length in direction a_1
L2           = {8, 12, 16}            ! Length in direction a_2
...
N_Phi        = [32, 72, 128; L2]            ! Total number of flux quanta traversing the lattice
...
Beta         = [8, 12, 16; L2]         ! Inverse temperature
...
Mz         = {.F., .T.}
....
ham_U      = [0.5d0, 1.5d0, 3.0d0, 5.0d0, 5.5d0, 6.0d0, 7.5d0]           ! Hubbard interaction

...
/

What would this simulation do?
L1           = ##
L2           = {8, 12, 16}
Investigate three lattice sizes:  L1 = L2 = {8, 12, 16}. We want a pi flux per Plaquette, as well as \beta = L.
Thus we need to set:
N_Phi        = [32, 72, 128; L2]
Beta         = [8, 12, 16; L2]
Therefore:
    - L2 = 8  ==> N_Phi = 32,  Beta = 8
    - L2 = 12 ==> N_Phi = 72,  Beta = 12
    - L2 = 16 ==> N_Phi = 128, Beta = 16

We would like to investigate
 


Some are excluded, as it does not make sense, because it would give double simulations:
&VAR_Max_Stoch
...
/

&VAR_errors
...
/


NOTICE, THAT THE RASTER IS always created for:
&VAR_lattice
&VAR_Model_Generic
(&VAR_QMC, which can be used for debugging/some monte carlo specific test, but here one should be careful..)
And the corresponding:
&VAR_ham_name
ham_name = "Hubbard"
/

We also allow to have e.g. {"Hubbard", "tV"}, which then will create a separate raster for each Model type!






